<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jun 30, 2022">
<CPscript title="The Discrete Riemann Mapping Theorem" >
<description>This script (an elaboration of "Cookie.xmd") illustrates the discrete Riemann Mapping Theorem and demonstrates the convergence of the discrete mappings to the classical under refinement. It uses grids and quasiconformal dilatation to guage fidelity.</description>
<text> The purpose of this script is described in Section 6.6 of the book; namely, to illustrate the convergence of discrete conformal maps to their classical counterparts. Here we map the disc to the Jordan region G shown in p1. </text>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_offline.png">act 1;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read RandDisc8000.p;infile_path kurve.g;disp -g; </cmd>
<text> The first section illustrates the basic 'cookie' construction of discrete conformal mappings. The following sections illustrate the quality of these maps, first with spoke-and-wheel grids and next with color coding for quasiconformal distortion. The commands are designed to work with various regions, background overlap packings, and refinement levels; the last section explains how you can run more general experiments.. </text>
<Section title="The Construction">
<text> The basic construction, described earlier in "cookie.xmd", is straightforward: (1) overlay G with some larger circle packing. (2) cookie out the shape of G, giving the range packing P in p1. (3) Construct the maximal packing Q as the domain in p0. The map f: Q --> P is a discrete conformal map with f(0) = 0.

Optional commands 'i' and 'd' let you increase or decrease the size of the overlaying packing: you have to do this before proceeding with the rest of the construction. </text>
<cmd tooltip="Overlay with a hexagonal packing" iconname="amarok.png">act 1;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd tooltip="'Cookie' out the packing of Omega" iconname="centrejust.png">act 1;cookie;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd tooltip="Create the max packing in the disc" iconname="psi.png">copy -p1 0;act 0;geom_to_h;set_rad -.1 b;repack;layout; Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<cmd tooltip="Color code boundary circles" iconname="xeyes.png">color -c s b;put_data -q1 -cc b;disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;disp -p1 -w -c -cf  b -g; </cmd>
<text> RESULT: The mapping between the maximal packing Q in p0 and the circle packing P in p1 is a "discrete conformal mapping" from the unit disc to G. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Level Curves">
<text> One way to judge the fidelity of a discrete map is to superimpose spoke-and-wheel grid. The grid is laid down over the maximal packing with 'set_grid'. It can then be carried forward to any euclidean packing of the same complex and displayed with 'disp -b'. A comparison with the grid generated by Schwarz-Christoffel numerical mapping is shown in Figure 6.9 of the book. </text>
<cmd name="g" mnemonic="g" iconname="network.png">set_grid -p0;set_brush -p0 0;disp -p0 -wr;disp -p0 -bc5t3;set_brush -p1 0;disp -p1 -wr;disp -p1 -bc5t3 </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Quasiconformal Distortion">
<text> A second way to judge fidelity of a discrete map is to compute the quasiconformal dilatation k as described in the book. The 'k' command color codes the faces of P to reflect the local dilatation using red and blue: faces with dilatations over a threshold (specified in the 'color' command) are.blue; those with dilatations k below the threshold will be red, becoming less red as k goes down to 1. (A high threshold and the reds are too uniform: keep the threshold only slightly larger than 1.) </text>
<cmd name="k" mnemonic="k" tooltip="color code for quasiconformal dilatation" iconname="run.png">copy -p0 2;geom_to_e -p2;color -p1 -f qc -q2 -m 1.25 a;disp -p1 -w -ffc -g; </cmd>
<text> If the discrete mappings converge (uniformly on compact subsets of the disc) to the classical conformal map, then under refinement the red should wash out in more and more of the interior. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Experimentation">
<text> The user can experiment quite widely with the tools provided here. There are a number of overlay packings included along with additional Jordan curves --- and you can always draw on the canvas (see the "Cursors" menu) or read a curve of your choosing from a file (see 'read_path'). 

To change the overlay and/or curve requires that you change the first command (the '*' command). After that, you can adjust the refinement level with 'i' and 'd', create the grid with 'g', and color code the distortion with 'k'.

A good way to see the convergence is to color code a succession of refinements, taking a screenshot of each, then stitch the jpg's into a movie (see the 'Screen --> Screen Shot' window).

Enjoy! </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="i" mnemonic="i" tooltip="increase all radii" iconname="icq_dnd.png">adjust_rad -p1 1.2 a;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" tooltip="decrease all radii" iconname="format_increaseindent.png">adjust_rad -p1 .9 a;layout -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -g; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
    <AboutImage name="AboutImage.jpg">

    </AboutImage>
